> [!MARKS] 3 + 5 = 8 Marks (`a`: Header file & C comments — 3m · `b`: Bitwise operators + `break` & `goto` with example — 5m)

### Part (a) — Header Files and Comment Statements in C

- **Header File (`.h`):** A text file containing function prototypes, symbolic macro definitions (`#define`), structure declarations, and `typedef` aliases shared across `.c` source files via the preprocessor `#include` directive:
  - **System Header (`<...>`):** `#include <stdio.h>` searches standard compiler include directories (declares `printf()`, `scanf()`, `FILE`).
  - **User Header (`"..."`):** `#include "mymath.h"` searches the current project directory first.
- **Comment Statements in C:** Ignored completely by the compiler (replaced by a single space during preprocessing) to document logic:
  - **Multi-line / Block Comment (ANSI C89):** `/* ... */` — spans one or multiple lines; cannot be nested.
  - **Single-line Comment (C99+):** `// ...` — runs from `//` to the end of that physical line.

```c
#include <stdio.h>   // Single-line C99 comment: standard I/O header
/* Multi-line C89 comment:
   Demonstrates header inclusion and comments */
```

### Part (b) — Bitwise Operators, `break`, and `goto`

- **Bitwise Operators:** Operate directly on individual binary bits of integer types (`char`, `int`, `unsigned`, `long`) — never `float` or `double`:

| Operator | Meaning | Example (`a = 6 = 0110₂`, `b = 3 = 0011₂`) | Result |
|---|---|---|---|
| `&` | Bitwise AND | `6 & 3` (`0110 & 0011`) | `2` (`0010₂`) |
| `|` | Bitwise OR | `6 | 3` (`0110 ¦ 0011`) | `7` (`0111₂`) |
| `^` | Bitwise XOR | `6 ^ 3` (`0110 ^ 0011`) | `5` (`0101₂`) |
| `~` | One's Complement | `~6` (inverts all bits; `-x - 1` in 2's comp.) | `-7` |
| `<<` / `>>` | Left / Right Shift | `6 << 1` / `6 >> 1` | `12` / `3` |

- **`break` Statement:** Immediately exits the innermost enclosing `for`, `while`, `do-while` loop, or `switch` block, transferring control to the first statement following it.
- **`goto` Statement:** Performs an unconditional jump (`goto label;`) to a labelled statement (`label: statement;`) inside the **same function** (useful for breaking out of deeply nested loops or jumping to centralized error cleanup).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    for (int i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
        if (i == 3) {
            break;              /* exits loop when i == 3 */
        }
        printf("%d ", i);
    }
    goto done;                  /* jumps forward to label 'done' */
    printf("Skipped\n");
done:
    printf("-> Jumped to done\n");
    return 0;
}
/* Output: 1 2 -> Jumped to done */
```

> [!MARKS] 2 + 3 + 3 = 8 Marks (`a`: Nested `if-else` — 2m · `b`: `while` vs `do-while` — 3m · `c`: C program for GCD — 3m)

### Part (a) — Nested `if-else` Statement

- **Concept:** A nested `if-else` places an entire `if` or `if-else` construct inside the `if` body or `else` body of an outer `if` statement to test hierarchical multi-stage conditions.
- **Dangling-`else` Rule:** An unbraced `else` always pairs with the nearest preceding unmatched `if` in the same block.

```c
if (marks >= 40) {
    if (marks >= 75)
        printf("Distinction\n");
    else
        printf("Pass\n");
} else {
    printf("Fail\n");
}
```

### Part (b) — `while` Loop vs `do-while` Loop

> [!COMPARE] `while` Loop vs `do-while` Loop in C
> | Parameter | `while` Loop | `do-while` Loop |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Control Type** | Entry-controlled (pre-test) loop. | Exit-controlled (post-test) loop. |
> | **Condition Check** | Tested **before** executing the loop body on each pass. | Tested **after** executing the loop body on each pass. |
> | **Minimum Executions** | `0` times if the condition is initially false (`0`). | At least **`1` time**, even if the condition is initially false. |
> | **Syntax & Semicolon** | `while (cond) { ... }` — no trailing semicolon after `while (cond)`. | `do { ... } while (cond);` — mandatory semicolon `;` at the end. |

### Part (c) — C Program to Calculate GCD of Two Numbers (Euclidean Algorithm)

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a, b, rem;
    printf("Enter two positive integers: ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &a, &b) != 2) return 1;
    if (a < 0) a = -a;
    if (b < 0) b = -b;

    while (b != 0) {
        rem = a % b;
        a = b;
        b = rem;
    }
    printf("GCD = %d\n", a);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace (`a = 48, b = 18`):** `(48, 18) -> rem = 12 -> (18, 12) -> rem = 6 -> (12, 6) -> rem = 0 -> (6, 0)` → prints `GCD = 6`.

> [!MARKS] 3 + 5 = 8 Marks (`a`: `switch-case` explanation & example — 3m · `b`: C program for triangular-sum series `s` — 5m)

### Part (a) — `switch-case` Multi-Way Branching in C

- **Mechanism:** `switch (expr)` evaluates an **integer or character expression** (`int`, `char`, `enum` — never `float`, `double`, or strings) and jumps directly to the matching constant `case` label.
- **Role of `break` & `default`:** Without `break;`, execution **falls through** sequentially into subsequent `case` blocks until a `break;` or closing brace is reached; `default:` is optional and catches all unmatched values.
- **Compile-Time Rules:** Every `case` label must be a **unique integer/character constant expression** known at compile time (duplicate `case` values or variable labels cause a compiler error).

```c
switch (day) {
    case 1:  printf("Monday\n");  break;
    case 2:  printf("Tuesday\n"); break;
    default: printf("Other day\n"); break;
}
```

### Part (b) — C Program for `s = 1 + (1+2) + (1+2+3) + ... + (1+2+...+n)`

- **Series Insight:** The `i`-th bracketed group is the running triangular partial sum `term = 1 + 2 + ... + i` (`term += i`), and `s` accumulates `s += term` in `O(n)` single-loop time (closed form `n(n+1)(n+2)/6`).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int n, i;
    long term = 0, s = 0;

    printf("Enter n: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n < 1) {
        printf("Enter a positive integer.\n");
        return 1;
    }

    for (i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        term += i;      /* term = 1 + 2 + ... + i */
        s += term;      /* accumulate into total series sum */
    }

    printf("Sum of series s = %ld\n", s);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace (`n = 4`):**

| Iteration `i` | `term` (`1+...+i`) | Accumulated `s` |
|---|---|---|
| `1` | `1` | `1` |
| `2` | `1 + 2 = 3` | `1 + 3 = 4` |
| `3` | `3 + 3 = 6` | `4 + 6 = 10` |
| `4` | `6 + 4 = 10` | `10 + 10 = 20` |

- **Sample Output (`n = 4`):** `Sum of series s = 20`.

> [!MARKS] 4 + 4 = 8 Marks (`a`: `strcpy()` and `strcat()` — 4m · `b`: C program for string palindrome check — 4m)

### Part (a) — String Handling Functions `strcpy()` and `strcat()` (`<string.h>`)

- **`char *strcpy(char *dest, const char *src)`:**
  - Copies every character from `src` (including the terminating `'\0'` null byte) into `dest` and returns `dest`.
  - Example: `char d[20]; strcpy(d, "Code");` sets `d` to `"Code"`.
- **`char *strcat(char *dest, const char *src)`:**
  - Finds the terminating `'\0'` of `dest`, overwrites it with the first character of `src`, appends the rest of `src` plus a new `'\0'`, and returns `dest`.
  - Example: `char d[20] = "C "; strcat(d, "Lang");` yields `"C Lang"`.

> [!TRAP] Buffer Capacity Requirement
> Neither `strcpy()` nor `strcat()` performs automatic bounds checking: `dest` must have capacity `>= strlen(src) + 1` for `strcpy()` and `>= strlen(dest) + strlen(src) + 1` for `strcat()` to avoid buffer overflow (prefer bounded `strncpy()` / `strncat()` or `snprintf()` when input length is untrusted, and never pass overlapping buffers).

### Part (b) — C Program to Check Whether an Input String Is a Palindrome

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(void) {
    char str[100];
    int left = 0, right, is_pal = 1;

    printf("Enter a string: ");
    if (scanf("%99s", str) != 1) return 1;

    right = (int)strlen(str) - 1;
    while (left < right) {
        if (str[left] != str[right]) {
            is_pal = 0;
            break;
        }
        left++;
        right--;
    }

    if (is_pal)
        printf("\"%s\" is a Palindrome.\n", str);
    else
        printf("\"%s\" is Not a Palindrome.\n", str);

    return 0;
}
```

- **Dry-Run Trace (`str = "madam"`):** `(left=0, right=4): 'm'=='m'` → `(1, 3): 'a'=='a'` → `left=2 == right=2` stops → prints `"madam" is a Palindrome.` (`O(n)` time, `O(1)` extra space).

> [!MARKS] 4 + 4 = 8 Marks (`a`: Function definition + Library vs User-Defined Function — 4m · `b`: Recursive C program for factorial — 4m)

### Part (a) — Functions in C & Library vs User-Defined Functions

- **Definition:** A **function** in C is a self-contained, named block of statements designed to perform a specific sub-task, promoting modularity, code reuse, and easier debugging.

> [!COMPARE] Library Function vs User-Defined Function in C
> | Parameter | Library Function | User-Defined Function |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Author & Origin** | Pre-written and pre-compiled in the C standard library. | Written by the programmer inside `.c` source files. |
> | **Header Requirement** | Prototype supplied via standard headers (`<stdio.h>`, `<math.h>`, `<string.h>`). | Prototype and body must be declared and defined by the user. |
> | **Modifiability** | Fixed behaviour; programmer cannot alter internal library code. | Fully customizable logic, parameters, and return type. |
> | **Examples** | `printf()`, `scanf()`, `sqrt()`, `strlen()`, `malloc()`. | `factorial(n)`, `swap(&a, &b)`, `isPrime(x)`. |

### Part (b) — C Program to Find Factorial Using Recursion

- **Recurrence Relation:** Base case `0! = 1` and `1! = 1`; recursive step `n! = n * (n - 1)!` for `n >= 2`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

long long factorial(int n) {
    if (n <= 1) {
        return 1LL;                     /* base case terminates recursion */
    }
    return (long long)n * factorial(n - 1);
}

int main(void) {
    int n;
    printf("Enter a non-negative integer: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n < 0) {
        printf("Factorial is undefined for negative integers.\n");
        return 1;
    }
    printf("Factorial of %d = %lld\n", n, factorial(n));
    return 0;
}
```

- **Unwinding Trace (`n = 4`):** `factorial(4) = 4 * factorial(3) = 4 * (3 * factorial(2)) = 4 * (3 * (2 * factorial(1))) = 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 24`.

> [!MARKS] 3 + 5 = 8 Marks (`a`: Array definition, example & 1D declaration — 3m · `b`: C program to print matrix and its transpose — 5m)

### Part (a) — Array Definition and One-Dimensional Declaration

- **Definition:** An **array** in C is a fixed-size, contiguous block of memory locations storing homogeneous elements (all of the **same data type**) referenced through a common identifier and zero-based integer subscripts `0` to `N - 1`.
- **1D Declaration Syntax:** `<data_type> <array_name>[<size>];`
  - **Example:** `int marks[5] = {78, 85, 90, 66, 92};` allocates `5 * sizeof(int)` contiguous bytes where `marks[0] == 78` and `marks[4] == 92` (`address(marks[i]) = base + i * sizeof(int)`).

### Part (b) — C Program to Print a Matrix and Its Transpose

- **Transpose Rule:** For an `r x c` matrix `A`, its transpose `A^T` has dimensions `c x r` with entry `(A^T)[j][i] = A[i][j]`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a[10][10], r, c, i, j;

    printf("Enter rows and columns (max 10 10): ");
    if (scanf("%d %d", &r, &c) != 2 || r < 1 || r > 10 || c < 1 || c > 10)
        return 1;

    printf("Enter %d matrix elements:\n", r * c);
    for (i = 0; i < r; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < c; j++) {
            scanf("%d", &a[i][j]);
        }
    }

    printf("Original Matrix (%d x %d):\n", r, c);
    for (i = 0; i < r; i++) {
        for (j = 0; j < c; j++) {
            printf("%4d", a[i][j]);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }

    printf("Transpose Matrix (%d x %d):\n", c, r);
    for (j = 0; j < c; j++) {
        for (i = 0; i < r; i++) {
            printf("%4d", a[i][j]);
        }
        printf("\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Run (`2 x 3` input `1 2 3 / 4 5 6`):** Prints Original `2 x 3` rows `[1 2 3]`, `[4 5 6]` and Transpose `3 x 2` rows `[1 4]`, `[2 5]`, `[3 6]`.

> [!MARKS] 4 + 4 = 8 Marks (`a`: C program to sum array elements using pointer — 4m · `b`: Pointer definition + `malloc()`, `calloc()`, `free()` — 4m)

### Part (a) — C Program to Sum Array Elements Using a Pointer

- **Pointer Scale-Factor Rule:** Setting `int *ptr = arr;` points to `arr[0]`; advancing `ptr + i` steps forward by `i * sizeof(int)` bytes so `*(ptr + i)` accesses `arr[i]`.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int arr[100], n, i, sum = 0;
    int *ptr;

    printf("Enter number of elements (max 100): ");
    if (scanf("%d", &n) != 1 || n < 1 || n > 100) return 1;

    printf("Enter %d integers: ", n);
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        scanf("%d", &arr[i]);
    }

    ptr = arr;                      /* points to arr[0] */
    for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        sum += *(ptr + i);          /* dereference i-th element */
    }

    printf("Sum of array elements = %d\n", sum);
    return 0;
}
```

- **Sample Output (`n = 4`, input `10 20 30 40`):** `Sum of array elements = 100`.

### Part (b) — Pointer Definition & Dynamic Memory Functions (`<stdlib.h>`)

- **Pointer:** A derived variable whose value is the **memory address** of another typed variable (declared as `int *p = &x;`, where `&` is address-of and `*` is indirection/dereference).
- **`malloc(size_t size)`:** Allocates **one contiguous heap block** of `size` uninitialized bytes (contains garbage) and returns `void *` (`NULL` if out of memory), e.g., `int *p = malloc(5 * sizeof *p);`.
- **`calloc(size_t n, size_t elem_size)`:** Allocates heap memory for an array of `n` elements of `elem_size` bytes each and **initializes every byte to `0`**, e.g., `int *p = calloc(5, sizeof *p);`.
- **`free(void *ptr)`:** Releases a heap block previously obtained from `malloc()`, `calloc()`, or `realloc()` back to the heap manager to prevent memory leaks (set `ptr = NULL;` afterward to avoid dangling pointers).

> [!MARKS] 3 + 2 + 3 = 8 Marks (`a`: Ternary operator — 3m · `b`: Actual vs formal parameter — 2m · `c`: Leap-year C program — 3m)

### Part (a) — Ternary (Conditional) Operator (`? :`)

- **Syntax & Arity:** `condition ? expr_true : expr_false` is C's **only ternary operator** (takes three operands and associates right-to-left).
- **Short-Circuit Evaluation:** `condition` is evaluated first; if non-zero (true), **only** `expr_true` is evaluated and returned; if zero (false), **only** `expr_false` is evaluated and returned.

```c
int a = 15, b = 22;
int max = (a > b) ? a : b;   /* evaluates to 22 */
```

### Part (b) — Actual Parameter vs Formal Parameter

> [!COMPARE] Actual Parameter vs Formal Parameter in C
> | Parameter | Actual Parameter (Argument) | Formal Parameter |
> |---|---|---|
> | **Location** | Written inside the **function call** statement (`add(x, 5)`). | Declared inside the **function header/definition** (`int add(int a, int b)`). |
> | **Allowed Form** | Can be constants, variables, or arbitrary expressions (`x + 2`). | Must be typed variable identifiers (`int a`, `int *p`). |
> | **Memory Scope** | Belongs to the calling function's scope. | Local variable created on the called function's stack frame. |

### Part (c) — C Program to Check Leap Year

- **Gregorian Calendar Rule:** A year `y` is a leap year if it is divisible by `400`, **or** divisible by `4` and **not** divisible by `100` (so `2000` and `2024` are leap years, while `1900` is not).

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int year;
    printf("Enter a year: ");
    if (scanf("%d", &year) != 1) return 1;

    if ((year % 400 == 0) || (year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0))
        printf("%d is a Leap Year.\n", year);
    else
        printf("%d is Not a Leap Year.\n", year);

    return 0;
}
```
